                <pb facs="0516" n="434" sameAs="frus:frus1911/pg434"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 812.00/1012.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of
                            State to the Mexican
                    Ambassador.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1911-03-24"><hi rendition="#italic">March 24,
                        1911</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Excellency</hi>: With reference to the Department’s
                    reply of yesterday to your excellency’s communication of the 20th instant
                    inquiring as to Gen. Luque’s desire to purchase certain supplies on this side of
                    the frontier, and with reference also to the duties of American customs
                    officials in connection with such a transaction, I have the honor to point out
                    to your excellency that such officials could be concerned in the ordinary course
                    of their duties only with the proper enforcement of existing regulations,
                    whereby exports and imports may only be effected at an established port of
                    entry.</p>
                <p>Accept, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Huntington
                    Wilson.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
